1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,693
As we saw in the last lecture,

2
00:00:02,693 --> 00:00:11,540
a major cause of climate change is the rapid rise in the level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere over the last century.

3
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:14,900
If we could reduce the amount of CO2

4
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:18,930
perhaps the rate of climate change could also be slowed down.

5
00:00:18,930 --> 00:00:25,080
One potential method involves enhancing the role of the soil that plants grow in

6
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:27,520
with regard to absorbing CO2.

7
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:32,981
Rattan Lai, a soil scientist from Ohio State University, in the USA,

8
00:00:32,981 --> 00:00:41,174
claims that the world's agricultural soils could potentially absorb 13 percent of the carbon dioxide in the atmosphere -

9
00:00:41,174 --> 00:00:46,180
the equivalent of the amount released in the last 30 years.

10
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:50,070
And research is going on into how this might be achieved.

11
00:00:50,070 --> 00:00:55,460
Lai first came to the idea that soil might be valuable in this way

12
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:57,799
not through an interest in climate change,

13
00:00:57,799 --> 00:01:03,320
but rather out of concern for the land itself and the people dependent on it.

14
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:10,180
Carbon-rich soil is dark, crumbly and fertile, and retains some water.

15
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:13,820
But erosion can occur if soil is dry

16
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:18,470
which is a likely effect if it contains inadequate amounts of carbon.

17
00:01:18,470 --> 00:01:25,470
Erosion is of course bad for people trying to grow crops or breed animals on that terrain.

18
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:30,829
In the 1970s and '80s, Lai was studying soils in Africa

19
00:01:30,829 --> 00:01:37,650
so devoid of organic matter that the ground had become extremely hard, like cement.

20
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:41,945
There he met a pioneer in the study of global warming,

21
00:01:41,945 --> 00:01:46,630
who suggested that carbon from the soil had moved into the atmosphere.

22
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:51,690
This is now looking increasingly likely. Let me explain.

23
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:57,791
For millions of years, carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been regulated,

24
00:01:57,791 --> 00:02:02,520
in part, by a natural partnership between plants and microbes

25
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:04,830
tiny organisms in the soil.

26
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:08,580
Plants absorb CO2 from the air

27
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:13,230
and transform it into sugars and other carbon-based substances.

28
00:02:13,230 --> 00:02:17,744
While a proportion of these carbon products remain in the plant,

29
00:02:17,744 --> 00:02:22,180
some transfer from the roots to fungi and soil microbes

30
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,720
which store the carbon in the soil.

31
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,920
The invention of agriculture some 10

32
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,749
000 years ago disrupted these ancient soil-building processes

33
00:02:31,749 --> 00:02:36,770
and led to the loss of carbon from the soil.

34
00:02:36,770 --> 00:02:40,060
When humans started draining the natural topsoil,

35
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:46,170
and ploughing it up for planting, they exposed the buried carbon to oxygen.

36
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:51,420
This created carbon dioxide and released it into the air.

37
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:58,120
And in some places, grazing by domesticated animals has removed all vegetation

38
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:00,170
releasing carbon into the air.

39
00:03:00,170 --> 00:03:04,718
Tons of carbon have been stripped from the world's soils -

40
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:10,630
where it's needed - and pumped into the atmosphere. So what can be done?

41
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:12,676
Researchers are now coming up

42
00:03:12,676 --> 00:03:20,860
with evidence that even modest changes to farming can significantly help to reduce the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.

43
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:27,500
Some growers have already started using an approach known as regenerative agriculture.

44
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,920
This aims to boost the fertility of soil

45
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:34,490
and keep it moist through established practices.

46
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:38,680
These include keeping fields planted all year round

47
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:42,400
and increasing the variety of plants being grown.

48
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:48,492
Strategies like these can significantly increase the amount of carbon stored in the soil,

49
00:03:48,492 --> 00:03:55,020
so agricultural researchers are now building a case for their use in combating climate change.

50
00:03:55,020 --> 00:04:08,410
One American investigation into the potential for storing CO2 on agricultural lands is taking place in California.

51
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:12,863
Soil scientist Whendee Silver of the University of California,

52
00:04:12,863 --> 00:04:19,790
Berkeley, is conducting a first-of-its-kind study on a large cattle farm in the state.

53
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:28,479
She and her students are testing the effects on carbon storage of the compost that is created from waste -

54
00:04:28,479 --> 00:04:31,222
both agricultural, including manure and cornstalks,

55
00:04:31,222 --> 00:04:36,710
and waste produced in gardens, such as leaves, branches, and lawn trimmings.

56
00:04:36,710 --> 00:04:45,040
In Australia, soil ecologist Christine Jones is testing another promising soil-enrichment strategy.

57
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:54,750
Jones and 12 farmers are working to build up soil carbon by cultivating grasses that stay green all year round.

58
00:04:54,750 --> 00:04:58,682
Like composting, the approach has already been proved experimentally;

59
00:04:58,682 --> 00:05:04,361
Jones now hopes to show that it can be applied on working farms

60
00:05:04,361 --> 00:05:08,730
and that the resulting carbon capture can be accurately measured.

61
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:15,114
It's hoped in the future that projects such as these will demonstrate the role that farmers

62
00:05:15,114 --> 00:05:20,700
and other land managers can play in reducing the harmful effects of greenhouse gases.

63
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,593
For example, in countries like the United States,

64
00:05:24,593 --> 00:05:28,973
where most farming operations use large applications of fertiliser,

65
00:05:28,973 --> 00:05:33,840
changing such long-standing habits will require a change of system.

66
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:38,180
Rattan Lai argues that farmers should receive payment

67
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:40,512
not just for the corn or beef they produce,

68
00:05:40,512 --> 00:05:44,830
but also for the carbon they can store in their soil.

69
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:49,000
Another study being carried out...
